<script>
import { url } from '@abide/abide'
import Doc from '#src/docs/Doc.abide'
import Example from '#src/docs/Example.abide'
import Section from '#src/docs/Section.abide'
import Chart from './Chart.abide'
import Live from './Live.abide'
import Orders from './Orders.abide'
import chartSource from './Chart.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import liveSource from './Live.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import ordersSource from './Orders.abide' with { type: 'text' }
</script>

<Doc>
    <Section title="The two caps, and what they cap">
        <p>Both cap <strong>how often the value changes</strong>, and both apply however the change
        was driven.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th></th><th>Fires</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>throttle: n</code></td><td>immediately, then at most once per <code>n</code> ms</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>debounce: n</code></td><td>once the changes stop for <code>n</code> ms</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>Declaring both is a build error naming the value, there being no reading under which you
        meant one of them. Each value below takes them, and what drives the change is the producer's
        business:</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>The value</th><th>What moves it</th><th>What a window collapses</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>a derived value over a load</td><td>a trigger — a dependency, an <code>invalidate</code>, a <code>refresh</code>, a <code>ttl</code> lapsing</td><td>reloads</td></tr>
                <tr><td>a value you write</td><td>a write</td><td>wakes</td></tr>
                <tr><td>a channel</td><td>an append</td><td>wakes</td></tr>
                <tr><td>a stream — an <code>async function*</code> body</td><td>a chunk</td><td>wakes</td></tr>
                <tr><td>a keyed value — a body with a parameter</td><td>a key reached for the first time</td><td>starts</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`debounce` waits for the changes to stop">
        <Example
            id="cap-debounce"
            title={'`{ debounce }` — one load once the typing stops'}
            summary="A figure filtered by a region you type. Clear the box and type north, and watch the figure rather than the field."
            files={{
                'Orders.abide': ordersSource,
            }}
        >
            <Orders />
        </Example>

        <p><em>1 option, 0 markup changed</em> — it goes on the value that pays the cost, and the
        probes did not move either.</p>

        <p><code>debounce</code> is right where the intermediate values are not answers — a
        half-typed region being the example the whole page is built on. Five keystrokes are one load
        rather than five, and <strong>the count already held stays on screen the whole
        time.</strong></p>

        <p>That is the <code>refresh()</code> contract rather than a new one: while the window is
        holding a change back the value already held keeps being served, so the value stays
        <code>busy()</code> <strong>and</strong> <code>ready()</code> and a figure does not blank
        between keystrokes. A <strong>first</strong> load is busy with nothing to serve — which is
        the dash the card passes through once, on its way to the first figure.</p>

        <p>The hand-written arm is where the cost shows. The window is one timer, but it arrives
        with two things beside it: the flag that keeps the held figure up, and the guard that stops
        an answer for a region since typed past from landing on the page.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/values/show-a-value-that-isnt-there-yet')}>Loading states</a> ·
        <a href={url('/values/decide-when-a-value-reloads')}>Reloading</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`throttle` fires first and then rate-limits">
        <Example
            id="cap-throttle"
            title={'`{ throttle }` — the first arrival, then a ceiling'}
            summary="A feed arriving twenty times a second beside the tile that shows it. Watch the two figures drift apart."
            files={{
                'Live.abide': liveSource,
            }}
        >
            <Live />
        </Example>

        <p><code>throttle</code> is right where the value is being watched — a live count, a cursor
        position, anything where the first update should be immediate and the <strong>rate</strong>
        is the problem. The tile takes the first arrival at once, which is the half a debounce would
        have got wrong: a debounced value that never goes quiet shows nothing at all.</p>

        <p><strong>Both figures are right at every moment a reader looks</strong>, and only one of
        them cost a repaint per arrival. That is why the card's spec counts paints rather than
        comparing the two numbers — the value is identical either way, and the work is the whole
        claim.</p>

        <p>A window <strong>delays</strong>, and does not itself drop (5.9). It collapses a batch
        into one wake, so a capped channel tells its readers once per window, and a
        <code>{'{#for}'}</code> over it keyed with <code>by</code> paints every message the window
        held in one go rather than one per frame (5.23). For a value that is replaced rather than
        appended to, the batch is the latest value, which is why the same rule looks like two
        behaviors.</p>

        <p>What survives the batch is what the value holds, never the window. There is no second
        buffer behind a cap, so a capped value written three times in one window serves the third
        and the first two are gone. Where the ones between are the point, append them to an array
        and trim it.</p>

        <p>A stream takes either cap too, and the window is over how often its
        <strong>readers</strong> wake (5.17). A read serves the accumulation as the last release
        left it, not the chunks the window is still holding (5.18), while a cursor still gets every
        chunk (5.10). The close releases what the window holds, so <code>await</code> resolves with
        the whole accumulation (2.7).</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/values/keep-the-last-few-values')}>Recent values</a> ·
        <a href={url('/data/send-data-as-it-arrives')}>Streaming data</a> ·
        <a href={url('/values/let-anything-publish-and-anything-read')}>Channels</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="On a keyed value the window is over starting a key">
        <p>A body with a parameter is a space of values, and what a filter box drives through it is
        a new <strong>key</strong> per keystroke, not a new value under one key. So the window on a
        keyed value holds back <strong>starting</strong> a key it has not reached before (5.19).
        <code>{'search({ q })'}</code> reached for <code>n</code>, <code>no</code>, <code>nor</code>
        and <code>north</code> inside one debounce starts <code>north</code> alone. A throttle
        starts <code>n</code> at once and, at the end of the window, the latest key reached
        since.</p>

        <p>A key the window passed over for a later one is never started and never kept (5.20). No
        request goes out for <code>no</code>, and there is no entry for it left to hold. The key
        being held back reports <code>busy()</code> (5.21), so the page reads a load in flight,
        which is what it is about to be.</p>

        <p>A key already started is an ordinary entry. Reaching it again is a cache hit and does not
        wait. What moves it after that — a dependency, an <code>invalidate</code>, a
        <code>ttl</code> — is capped as any value is. A keyed channel is a keyed value, so reaching
        its key is held the same way, and after that the window is over its appends, as the table
        says.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/values/load-once-per-set-of-arguments')}>Caching</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="The cap belongs on the value, not at the call site">
        <p>A value that must not join a stampede says so about itself, where the knowledge is — a
        tag-wide <code>{'invalidate({ tags: [\'invoice\'] })'}</code> cannot know what else it
        matched, so it is the wrong place to decide how hard any one entry may be hit.</p>

        <p>The one read that ignores the cap is a <code>bind:</code>. The binding wrote the value,
        so it sees its own write immediately while the rest of the page waits out the window —
        otherwise a throttled value under <code>bind:value</code> would snap the text back under a
        typist between keystrokes.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/values/load-once-per-set-of-arguments')}>Caching</a> ·
        <a href={url('/templates/bind-a-form-to-state')}>Form binding</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`identity` wakes readers only when the value moved">
        <Example
            id="identity-structural"
            title="`identity: structural` — an equal reload wakes nobody"
            summary="Two regional totals and the chart drawn from them. Record a sale and the chart redraws; refresh with nothing new and it does not."
            files={{
                'Chart.abide': chartSource,
            }}
        >
            <Chart />
        </Example>

        <p>The other half of "too fast" is a value that changes without changing. Every load hands
        back a new record, so by reference each Refresh is a change, and the chart redraws to show
        the numbers it was already showing. <code>identity: structural</code> compares what landed
        with what was held, and an equal record wakes nobody — the redraw count stands still. Record
        a sale first to see it move: the count is live, and the comparison is what holds it.</p>

        <p><code>identity</code> decides what counts as the same value, and getting it wrong leaves
        the output right and the work wrong: nothing renders incorrectly, the page just re-renders
        on every recompute.</p>

        <p><strong>The reference is the default</strong> (5.4), which is what JavaScript means by
        the same value. Declare <code>identity: structural</code> on the value whose producer
        rebuilds what it hands back — a reload that fetches an equal record, a list rebuilt from the
        same rows — and it collapses without waking anybody. It is not the default because the walk
        runs on every write, where the wake it saves is collected only when the payload really is
        equal.</p>

        <p>It BAILS rather than guesses: a <code>Map</code>, a <code>Set</code>, a class instance, a
        cycle, and it answers "not equal", so an indecisive comparison wakes readers instead of
        collapsing a change. <code>canonical</code> is not this and is not an equality at all; it
        builds a keyed value's KEY, which has to be stable across processes and across sides, so it
        sorts, drops <code>undefined</code> and allocates a string.</p>

        <p>Write your own comparator where <code>structural</code> bails on a shape you need
        compared. It takes either form: a <strong>projection</strong> compared with
        <code>!==</code>, or a <strong>comparator</strong> answering directly. The second exists
        because "same length, same elements" cannot be projected — a freshly built array is
        <code>!==</code> whatever is in it.</p>

        <p>A value you write holding an object is the case you hit first. A form binding rewrites it
        per keystroke, a <code>refresh()</code> on <code>reactive(() =&gt; fetchUser())</code>
        returns a structurally identical user, and a write through a member path copies down the
        path. Each of them is a new reference for a value that did not move.</p>

        <p>Every write is gated, a channel's included (5.2). A message said twice is still a change
        there: it is a second element in the array rather than the same value written again.
        <code>identity</code> decides when two values are one, and an append is never the same value
        as the array before it.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/values/let-anything-publish-and-anything-read')}>Channels</a> ·
        <a href={url('/values/derive-a-value-from-other-values')}>Derived values</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`identity` costs O(size) per production">
        <p>Walking is O(size) per production — per <code>set</code>, per recompute — and it is the
        cost the default accepts. A four-key record is 48 ns, and a hundred rows rebuilt over the
        same row references is 373 ns because every shared element settles on the first check. Five
        thousand rows all freshly built and all equal is 0.31 ms, which is the pathological end and
        still under 2% of a frame. The walk that finds its difference at the last row is the one
        that pays without collecting.</p>

        <p>It compares the <strong>stored</strong> value against the previous one, which is why it
        never runs per chunk — a streaming producer has one stored value, the accumulation,
        materialized on the read that asks for it (9.1). It holds one token and not a set, so it
        compares against the previous stored value alone (9.3), and a value that keeps a long array
        is compared against one array, not against every value it ever held.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Next">
        <ul>
            <li><a href={url('/values/load-once-per-set-of-arguments')}>Caching</a> — the entries a
            cap applies to</li>
            <li><a href={url('/values/keep-the-last-few-values')}>Recent values</a> — an array
            appended to and trimmed in one patch</li>
            <li><a href={url('/templates/bind-a-form-to-state')}>Form binding</a> — the input the
            filter came from</li>
        </ul>
    </Section>
</Doc>
